  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front will continue to dig south through\nearly this morning, bringing a round of showers.\n\nA cold front continues to slide south over the region this\nmorning, and a round of showers are sliding across Delmarva and\nsouthern New Jersey. There is still ample low level moisture as\nevidenced by the 70+ degree dew points ahead of the front, but \nlift is on the weaker side, and instability is quite limited. \nGiven radar trends however, issued a quick update to increase \nthe PoPs quite a bit early this morning across Delmarva and \nacross portions of southern New Jersey. Some moderate to briefly\nheavy downpours will occur at times. Also, increased the cloud \ncover for a time.\n\nFollowing any morning showers, today will shape up to be a nice\nday with clouds breaking later in the day. Highs in the upper \n70s to low 80s in most areas.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A low approaching the area Saturday into Sunday\nwill bring a warm front followed close behind by a cold front.\n\nOnce the cold front this morning slides south of our region, our\nattention next turns to the Saturday/Sunday time period when\nfirst a warm front will lift through the region on Saturday,\nfollowed closely by a cold front Saturday Night into Sunday. The\nexact evolution of the mid level short wave trough and surface\nlow or trough is still uncertain. Regardless, there should be\nplenty of lift, even just with the fronts, for widespread\nshowers and thunderstorms, particularly ahead of the cold front\nSaturday Night into Sunday.\n\nAs for the hazards, the main threat remains heavy rain and flash\nflooding. Model soundings continue to indicate precipitable\nwater values well above the 90th percentile and even at or\nexceeding many of the daily max values for surrounding sounding\nlocations. Model soundings also show remarkably deep warm cloud\nlayers (for mid September), further increasing the concern for\nheavy rain. The big questions at this point are how quickly are\nthe fronts (and thus storms) going to move through our region, \nand where will the axis of heaviest rain be. Given that we've\nhad at this point a week of little rain, rivers are in pretty \ngood shape in terms of lower water levels limiting the concern \nfor river flooding. The bigger concern with this event will be \nflash flooding, particularly if the heavier rains fall in our \nmore flash flood prone urban areas, and flooding on smaller\ncreeks and streams.\n\nGiven the forecasted timing of this event, instability looks to\nbe quite limited. Thus the threat for severe thunderstorm\nhazards (damaging winds, large hail, and tornadoes) is very\nlimited. \n\nIn the wake of the cold front on Sunday, drier air should start\nto settle in to the area. A few models are depicting a secondary\ncold front moving through the region Sunday Night. That could\nbring some isolated showers to the region, but given the drier\nair in place, the vast majority of the region should stay dry.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...During the later half of next week, a \nstationary front may set up just north of our region.\n\nOnce we get through this weekend, attention will turn to\nThursday and Friday of next week. Models are depicting a\nstationary front setting up north of our region. The northern\nhalf of our region already has 40 to 60% chance for showers and\nstorms through this period. However, of bigger note, the pattern\nhas similarities to the Maddox frontal event heavy rain pattern.\nGiven how it is currently depicted on most models, even if the\nsimilarities continue, the main area of heavy rain rain would be\nnorth of our region. However, if we see any trends of the front\nsetting up further south, that could increase the risk for heavy\nrain within our region.",
